          %h1#moore_closures Moore closures
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions"} Definitions
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#InTermsOfClosureCondition"} In terms of closure condition
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#InTermsOfClosureOperators"} In terms of closure operators
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#InTermsOfMonads"} In terms of monads
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#generalisations"} Generalisations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em Moore closure
            is a very general idea of what it can mean for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            to be
            %em closed
            under some condition. It includes, as special cases, the operation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closure
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base"} bases
            and even
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subbase"} subbases
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subalgebras"} subalgebras
            from subsets of an algebra.
          %p
            Secretly, it is the same thing as the collection of subsets preserved by some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            (the subset of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"}> modal types
            ”). In fact it is a special case of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+operator"} closure operator
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrine"} hyperdoctrine
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p We give two equivalent definitions. The first one
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#InTermsOfClosureCondition"} In terms of closure condition
          %p
            gives the explicit condition for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            to qualify as a Moore closure, the second
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#InTermsOfClosureOperators"} In terms of closure operators
          %p
            characterizes Moore closures as the collections of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
            of suitable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+operators"} closure operators
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#InTermsOfMonads"} In terms of monads
          %p
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+lattice"} subobject lattice
            of the given set.
          %h3#InTermsOfClosureCondition In terms of closure condition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} \subset \mathcal{P}X
              be a collection of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a
              %strong Moore collection
              if every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
              of members of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              belongs to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %p
              That is, given a family
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A_i)_i
              of sets in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall i,\; A_i \in \mathcal{C} \;\Rightarrow\; \bigcap_i A_i \in \mathcal{C} .
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given any collection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              whatsoever of subsets of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %strong generated
              by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              is the collection of all intersections of members of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This is indeed a Moore collection, and it equals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              is a Moore collection.
          %h3#InTermsOfClosureOperators In terms of closure operators
          #ClosureOperator.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Again let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a set, and now let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl
              be an operation on subsets of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl
              is a
              %strong closure operation
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl
              is isotone, extensive, and idempotent. That is,
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq B \;\Rightarrow\; Cl(A) \subseteq Cl(B)
                ,
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq Cl(A)
                , and
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(Cl(A)) \subseteq Cl(A)
                (the reverse inclusion follows from the previous two properties).
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl
              is a closure operation, then let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be the collection of sets that equal their own closures (the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"}> modal types
              ” or “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+objects"}> local objects
              ”). Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a Moore collection.
            %p
              Conversely, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a Moore collection, then let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(A)
              be the intersection of all closed sets that contain
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl
              is a closure operator.
            %p Furthermore, the two maps above, from closure operators to Moore collections and vice versa, are inverses.
          %h3#InTermsOfMonads In terms of monads
          %p
            Moore closures on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are precisely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+lattice"} subobject lattice
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClosureOperator"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit
            and multiplication of the monad. (The rest of the requirements of a monad are trivial in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subsets"} closed subsets
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            form a Moore collection; here the closure of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is its closure in the usual sense. In fact, a topological space can be
            %em defined
            as a set equipped with a Moore closure with either of these additional properties (which are equivalent):
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(\empty) = \empty
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(A \cup B) = Cl(A) \cup Cl(B)
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
              is closed, and so is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              are closed.
          %p
            (However, these properties may fail in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %p
            The first pair of properties is equivalent to the following weaker ones given that
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq Cl(A)
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(\empty) \subseteq \empty
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi Cl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cl(A \cup B) \subseteq Cl(A) \cup Cl(B)
          %p
            The closure operator of a topological space with underlying set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+monad"} oplax monoidal monad
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join+semilattice"} join semilattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
            considered as a cocartesian monoidal category.
          %p
            Notice that a preclosure in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopological+space"} pretopological space
          %p Here are some algebraic examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                form a Moore collection; the closure of a subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is the subgroup generated by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %li
              %p
                The subrings of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                form a Moore collection; the closure of a subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is the subring generated by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %li
              %p
                The subspaces of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                form a Moore collection; the closure of a subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                is the subspace spanned by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %li
              %p
                OK, you get the idea. This applies to any
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} finitary algebraic theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+lattice"} algebraic lattice
          %p But also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroup
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                form a Moore collection; the closure of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                is the normal subgroup generated by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                form a Moore collection; the closure of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                is the ideal generated by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %li
              %p
                The (topologically) closed subspaces of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                form a Moore collection; the closure of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                is the closed subspace generated by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %li
              %p And many further examples.
          %p
            Here are some examples on
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The topologies on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                form a Moore collection on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
                is the topology generated by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                as a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subbase"} subbase
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                form a Moore collection on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                is the filter generated by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                as a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subbase"} subbase
                %em proper
                filters on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                do
                %em not
                form a Moore collection; not every
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                generates a proper filter.)
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-algebra"} algebras
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                form a Moore collection on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                is the
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℬ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
                = succeed "-algebra;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinals
            %li
              %p And so on.
          %p
            Topping off these, the Moore collections on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            form a Moore collection on
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            is the Moore collection generated by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            as described in the definitions.
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matroid"} matroid
          %h2#generalisations Generalisations
          %p
            The definition of Moore collection really makes sense in any
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inflattice"} inflattice
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} poset
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+operator"} closure operator
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Instead of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite poset
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝒫
                          %mi op
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}^{op}X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                form a Moore collection whose closure operator is the usual interior operation. Now we can define a topological space as a set equipped with a Moore closure operator on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mi op
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}^{op}X
                that preserves
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
                = succeed "s);" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
            %li
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \dashv g
                be a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+connection"} Galois connection
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} posets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %em normal
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(f(a)) \leq a
                (the reverse is always true). Then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ f
                is a closure operator. This generalises the case of the normal subgroups of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+group"} Galois group
                of an extension of
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
          %p
            Since Galois connections are simply
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matroid"} matroid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"}> modal operator
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"}> modal logic
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"} modal homotopy type theory
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Erich Schechter, Section 4.1-4.12 in:
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Analysis+and+its+Foundations"} Handbook of Analysis and its Foundations
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Closure_operator"} Closure operator
            %li
              %p
                B. Venkateswarlu, R. Vasu Babu , Getnet Alemu,
                %em Morphisms on Closure Spaces and Moore Spaces
                %a{:href => "https://www.ijpam.eu/contents/2014-91-2/6/6.pdf"} pdf
